hadiths[4]{hadithnumber,text}:
"863","Abu Jafar al-Mukharrimi Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Jafar narrated to us, from Shubah, from Qatadah, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a man who had drunk wine. He said: So he struck him with two palm stalks with him, about forty times. Then Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) did the like of that. When it was Umar (may Allah be pleased with him), he consulted the people, and Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf said: The lightest of the prescribed penalties is eighty."
"864","Abu Yahya Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahim al-Bazzar narrated to us, he said: Shabbabah informed us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a man who had drunk wine, so he struck him with two palm stalks forty times. And Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) did that. When it was Umar (may Allah be pleased with him), he consulted the people, and Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf said to him: The lightest of the prescribed penalties is eighty, so he did it."
"865","897 - Muhammad ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, from al-Harith - meaning Ibn Abd al-Rahman - from Abu Salama, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If he becomes drunk, flog him, three times, then he said on the fourth: then strike his neck."
"866","898 - Muhammad ibn Ismail al-Ahmasi narrated to us, he said: Waki narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abdullah ibn Murra, from Masruq, from Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The blood of a Muslim man who testifies that there is no god but Allah and that I am the Messenger of Allah is not lawful except for one of three persons: a life for a life, the married adulterer, and the one who abandons his religion and separates from the community."
